from physics_helpers import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'Write the equation linking density, mass and volume.','ρ = m/V.','4.3.1.1'),
q(1,'Name the change from solid directly to gas.','Sublimation.','4.3.1.2'),
q(1,'What is the unit of specific latent heat?','J/kg.','4.3.2.3'),
q(1,'What happens to the temperature of a pure substance while it melts at constant pressure?','It stays constant.','4.3.2.3'),
q(2,'Compare particle movement in a solid and a gas.','- In a solid particles **vibrate about fixed positions**.\n- In a gas particles move rapidly and **randomly in all directions**.','4.3.1.1'),
q(2,'Define internal energy.','The **total kinetic energy and potential energy of all the particles** in a system.','4.3.2.1','2023 1H Q03.4'),
q(2,'A block has mass 540 g and volume 200 cm³. Calculate its density in g/cm³.','ρ = m/V = 540/200 = **2.7 g/cm³**.','4.3.1.1','2022 1H Q02: density skills'),
q(2,'Why does a gas usually have much lower density than a liquid of the same substance?','- Gas particles are **much farther apart**.\n- The same mass occupies a much larger volume, so **mass per unit volume is lower**.','4.3.1.1'),
q(2,'An empty cylinder has mass 60 g. With 80 cm³ of liquid it has mass 124 g. Calculate the liquid’s density.','Liquid mass = 124 − 60 = **64 g**. Density = 64/80 = **0.80 g/cm³**.','4.3.1.1'),
q(2,'Explain why mass can decrease in an open beaker when water evaporates without violating conservation of mass.','- Water particles **escape into the surroundings** as vapour.\n- Total mass including the escaped vapour is conserved; particles are not destroyed.','4.3.1.2'),
q(2,'Why must the starting water volume be subtracted when measuring an irregular object’s volume?','- The final reading includes both the original water and the displaced volume.\n- **Final minus initial volume** gives the volume of the submerged object alone.','4.3.1.1','2022 1H Q02.1'),
q(3,'A 0.25 kg sample has specific heat capacity 800 J/kg°C. Calculate the energy needed to warm it from 20°C to 50°C.','- Δθ = 50 − 20 = **30°C**.\n- ΔE = mcΔθ = 0.25 × 800 × 30.\n- **6,000 J**.','4.3.2.2'),
q(3,'Calculate the energy required to melt 0.15 kg of ice at 0°C. Its specific latent heat of fusion is 334,000 J/kg.','- **E = mL**.\n- E = 0.15 × 334,000.\n- **50,100 J**.','4.3.2.3'),
q(3,'A sealed gas occupies 40 cm³ at 120 kPa. It expands to 60 cm³ at constant temperature. Calculate its new pressure.','- **p₁V₁ = p₂V₂**.\n- p₂ = 120 × 40/60.\n- **80 kPa**.','4.3.3.2'),
q(3,'Explain why heating a fixed mass of gas in a rigid sealed container increases its pressure.','- Average kinetic energy increases, so particles **move faster**.\n- Wall collisions occur more frequently and involve larger momentum changes.\n- The force per unit area increases, so **pressure rises**.','4.3.3.1','2023 1H Q09.3'),
q(3,'A bicycle pump becomes warm during rapid compression. Explain why.','- A force moves the piston, doing **work on the gas**.\n- Energy transfers into the gas’s **internal energy**.\n- With little time for energy to escape, average particle kinetic energy and **temperature increase**.','4.3.3.3'),
q(3,'Explain why internal energy increases while ice melts even though its temperature is constant.','- Energy is transferred into the ice by heating.\n- It increases particle **potential energy** as the arrangement and interactions change.\n- Average kinetic energy does not increase, so **temperature stays constant**.','4.3.2.1–4.3.2.3','2023 1H Q03.4'),
q(4,'A cuboid measures 4.0 cm × 3.0 cm × 2.0 cm and has mass 192 g. Calculate its density in g/cm³ and kg/m³.','- V = 4.0 × 3.0 × 2.0 = **24 cm³**.\n- ρ = 192/24 = **8.0 g/cm³**.\n- 1 g/cm³ = **1,000 kg/m³**.\n- Density = **8,000 kg/m³**.','4.3.1.1'),
q(4,'A gas is compressed slowly at constant temperature from 100 cm³ to 25 cm³. Its initial pressure is 90 kPa. Calculate the new pressure and explain the increase using particles.','- p₂ = p₁V₁/V₂ = 90 × 100/25 = **360 kPa**.\n- Average particle kinetic energy stays the same because **temperature is constant**.\n- Particles collide with the walls more frequently per unit area in the smaller volume.\n- Greater force per unit area means **greater pressure**.','4.3.3.2'),
q(4,'A 200 W heater melts 0.20 kg of ice at 0°C and warms the water to 10°C. Use L = 334,000 J/kg and c = 4,200 J/kg°C. Calculate the minimum heating time.','- Melting energy = mL = 0.20 × 334,000 = **66,800 J**.\n- Warming energy = mcΔθ = 0.20 × 4,200 × 10 = **8,400 J**.\n- Total energy = **75,200 J**.\n- t = E/P = 75,200/200 = **376 s**; real heat losses increase the time.','4.3.2.2–4.3.2.3'),
q(6,'Describe a method to determine the density of an irregular stone which does not dissolve in water. Include ways to obtain reliable measurements.','- Zero a balance and measure the dry stone’s **mass**.\n- Record the initial water volume in a measuring cylinder large enough for the stone.\n- Lower the stone gently until fully submerged, avoiding splashes and trapped air bubbles. Read the final volume at eye level.\n- Calculate stone volume from **final minus initial water volume**. A displacement can with collected water is an alternative.\n- Calculate **ρ = m/V** with consistent units.\n- Repeat and compare values; use suitably fine instrument scales and investigate anomalies. Repeats reduce random variation but do not correct a systematic zero error.','4.3.1.1','2022 1H Q02.1–02.4'),
q(6,'Explain how the energy of particles changes as a pure solid is heated, melts and then warms as a liquid. Refer to this heating curve.\n\n![Heating curve with slopes and state-change plateaus.](../Diagrams/heating-curve.svg)','- Initially the solid’s particles vibrate more rapidly as their **kinetic energy increases**, so temperature rises.\n- During melting the supplied energy changes the particle arrangement and increases **potential energy**.\n- Average kinetic energy stays constant during this stage, giving a **flat temperature section** even though internal energy increases.\n- Once all the solid has melted, further heating increases the average kinetic energy of the liquid’s particles, so temperature rises again.\n- **Internal energy is the total kinetic plus potential energy of all particles**. Use mcΔθ on warming sections and mL for the state change.\n- The particles themselves do not melt or expand into different particles; the material changes state.','4.3.2.1–4.3.2.3','2023 1H Q03.4')]
write_bank(3,items,'The sample includes a six-mark density investigation in 2022 Q02.1 and particle/internal-energy explanation in 2023 Q03.4. Gas-temperature reasoning appears in 2023 Q09.3. These examples support prioritising measurement, unit conversion and linked particle explanations; no recurrence claim is made for an individual topic seen in only one sampled paper. Other calculations practise the full current topic.')
